---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'How Do Ravens Find Food? Senses, Social Cues, and Recruitment';
const description = 'Ravens find food using keen vision and olfaction for local searching, and social information networks for locating distant large carcasses. The recruitment behaviour and information-centre hypothesis explained.';
const path        = '/questions/how-do-ravens-find-food/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'How do ravens find food?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How do ravens find food?', a: 'Ravens use keen eyesight for local searching and following other scavengers (especially wolves and eagles) to carcasses. They also use social information: when a raven discovers a large carcass too well-defended by territorial adults to access alone, juvenile ravens recruit others from communal roosts by using distinctive calls, then return to the carcass mob. This information-centre hypothesis was tested and developed by Bernd Heinrich in long-term Maine studies published in "Ravens in Winter" (1989).' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="id-page">
  <nav class="id-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Questions · Behaviour</p>
    <h1>How Do Ravens Find Food?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Senses, social information, and the recruitment puzzle</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens find food through <strong>visual search, following other scavengers</strong> (wolves, eagles, humans), and a sophisticated <strong>social information network</strong>. When a subordinate raven discovers a large guarded carcass, it recruits others from the communal roost using specific calls — the group then overwhelms territorial adults. This "information-centre hypothesis" was tested in multi-year field experiments by Bernd Heinrich (1989).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Direct Sensory Detection</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have excellent eyesight and use it for active scanning:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>High vantage points — soaring on thermals and scanning large areas</li>
      <li>Visual detection of carcasses from considerable distances, especially against snow</li>
      <li>Detecting aggregations of other scavengers (vultures circling, eagles perched) as indirect cues</li>
      <li>Smell: ravens have a relatively well-developed sense of smell for a bird, though vision remains primary for long-range detection</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In open country and in winter, ravens are highly effective long-range visual searchers.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Following Other Animals</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have a well-documented tendency to follow other animals that lead them to food:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Wolves:</strong> Ravens follow wolf packs and benefit from their kills; this relationship is widespread across the Holarctic range overlap. Wolves may tolerate or even benefit from ravens (early detection of carcasses, possible alerting function).</li>
      <li><strong>Human hunters:</strong> Ravens learn to follow gunshots in hunting season</li>
      <li><strong>Large raptors:</strong> Ravens mob eagles and large hawks over carcasses and sometimes follow them to new food sources</li>
      <li><strong>Vehicles:</strong> Ravens learn that road vehicles produce roadkill and patrol roads, particularly in winter</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This learned association with other predators and scavengers is a key foraging strategy — using other species as remote detectors of food resources.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Recruitment Puzzle and the Information-Centre Hypothesis</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> One of the most studied and puzzling aspects of raven foraging: why do ravens recruit other ravens to a carcass? Sharing food seems counter-productive.</p>
    <p>Heinrich's explanation, developed through multi-year field experiments in Maine:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Large carcasses in winter are defended by territorial adult raven pairs</li>
      <li>A single juvenile raven cannot access the carcass — it will be driven off by the territorial pair</li>
      <li>If the juvenile recruits a mob of 20–30 other juveniles from the communal roost, they can overwhelm the territorial pair's ability to defend</li>
      <li>The recruiting raven gives up exclusive access but gains access it would not otherwise have</li>
      <li>Communal roosts function as "information centres" — a raven that finds food recruits others; others follow; all benefit</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Heinrich tested this by offering carcasses to ravens, tracking individual marked birds, and running controlled experiments. The juvenile/adult territorial dynamic was key to the hypothesis.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Vocal Signals for Food</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens use specific vocalisations in food contexts:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A distinctive recruitment or "yelling" call that attracts other ravens over distances</li>
      <li>Food-associated calls that differ from alarm calls and territorial calls — nearby ravens respond differentially to these</li>
      <li>The specificity of food calls and their effect on audience behaviour has been studied in playback experiments</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Bugnyar & Kotrschal (2002) showed that ravens also modify calling behaviour depending on the audience — subordinates may suppress calls when dominant birds are within earshot to avoid having food location revealed.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Cache Retrieval</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens also "find" food by remembering where they stored it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They cache food in thousands of locations and retrieve it reliably months later</li>
      <li>Retrieval uses spatial memory and landmark cues</li>
      <li>They also recall the locations of others' caches and pirate them (scrounging)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is covered in detail in the <a href="/behaviour/raven-food-caching/">Raven Food Caching</a> chapter.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="id-sources id-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="id-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Heinrich, B. (1989). <em>Ravens in Winter</em>. Summit Books. — information-centre hypothesis; recruitment experiments; wolf-following.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Bugnyar, T. &amp; Kotrschal, K. (2002). Observational learning and the raiding of food caches in ravens (<em>Corvus corax</em>): is it 'tactical' deception? <em>Animal Behaviour</em>, 64(2), 185–195.</li>
      <li id="src-3">Bugnyar, T. &amp; Heinrich, B. (2005). Ravens, <em>Corvus corax</em>, differentiate between knowledgeable and ignorant competitors. <em>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</em>, 272, 1641–1646.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="id-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> peer-reviewed studies and field research</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="id-nav-bottom">
    <a href="/questions/what-do-ravens-eat/" class="id-back">← What Do Ravens Eat?</a>
    <a href="/behaviour/raven-food-caching/" class="id-next">Food Caching (full chapter) →</a>
  </nav>
</article>
